6.4  TCP, UDP and connections

TL;DR
  - TCP gives a reliable, ordered stream: it numbers the bytes, waits for
    acknowledgements and sends again what was lost.
  - UDP sends independent datagrams with no promise of delivery: no waiting,
    used by DNS, video calls, games and QUIC (HTTP/3).
  - A TCP connection starts with a three-way handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) and
    is named by four values: both addresses and both ports.

      ss -tan state established     # TCP connections open now
      ss -s                         # how many, in each state

On your machine
  L2 link   wlp0s20f3 up · neighbour 192.168.1.1 is f4:92:bf:0e:21:7a on
            wlp0s20f3
  L3 IP     docker0 172.17.0.1/16, wlp0s20f3 192.168.1.23/24, wlp0s20f3
            fe80::8ec6:81ff:fe2a:107f/64 · gateway 192.168.1.1 · forwards
            packets for others
  L4 TCP    12 connections open now; 30,120 started and 410 accepted since boot
            · 1,840 of 3.1 million segments resent
  L4 UDP    95,000 datagrams in, 61,000 out, since boot
  L7 DNS    127.0.0.53

Pitfalls
  - "Connection refused": the host answered, but nothing listens on that port.
    "Timed out": no answer at all, often a firewall dropping packets.
  - Many sockets in TIME_WAIT after closing is normal: the side that closed
    first keeps the pair for 60 seconds, so late packets can't confuse a new
    connection.
  - Retransmissions mean lost packets. A small share is normal, especially on
    Wi-Fi; a steady high rate slows every transfer.

Deep dive

  ▾ How does TCP know how fast to send?

    Congestion control: it starts slowly, speeds up while acknowledgements come
    back, and slows down when packets get lost. Linux uses CUBIC by default;
    BBR, which watches delays rather than losses, is a common alternative.
    sysctl net.ipv4.tcp_congestion_control shows yours.

  ▾ Why build QUIC on top of UDP?

    A new transport protocol can't cross the Internet: firewalls and routers let
    TCP and UDP through, little else. QUIC puts reliability, encryption and
    several streams on top of UDP, inside the application, so it can change
    without waiting for kernels and routers. And one lost packet no longer
    stalls every stream, as it does with HTTP/2 over TCP.

